    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-06-29-cursor-now-has-a-mobile-app-for-guiding-your-coding-agent-on",
      "title": "Cursor now has a mobile app for guiding your coding agent on the go",
      "summary": "Cursor has launched a new mobile app for remote oversight over coding agents.",
      "body": "10:03 AM PDT · June 29, 2026\nImage Credits:YouTube/Fortune (opens in a new window)\nCursor isn’t letting the $60 billion SpaceX acquisition slow it down.\nOn Monday, the company announced a new app called Cursor Mobile, designed for users who want to prompt coding agents directly from their phone. The app ties into the Cursor 2.0 changes unveiled in October, which shifted the service toward independent coding agents. With the mobile app, users can spin up new coding agents or interact with agents that were initiated from the desktop client.\nCursor’s move to mobile follows similar apps from Anthropic and OpenAI, which both offer ways to interact with their coding tools on mobile.\nIt’s part of a broader shift in AI-based coding tools, which are increasingly abstracting away from written code and toward oversight of code-writing agents. With no need to access large code bases, many developers are switching away from multi-monitor desktop setups in favor of phones, which allow continuous conversations with remote agents.\nIn a recent talk, Anthropic’s head of Claude Code Boris Cherny said he had almost entirely switched to mobile AI coding as a result. “Most of my coding now is on my phone,” Cherny said in the talk. “I would have said ‘you’re crazy’ if you told me that six months ago, but yeah, here we are.”\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
